This could become an excellent piece of absurdist phonetic montage: the word “new” gradually mutates through dozens of recognisable voices, accents, films, advertisements, scientific terms and memes until it becomes a cat meowing.

The trick is to treat it as a sound gradient, rather than just a collection of words beginning with N.

There are two important pronunciations:

- British new = /njuː/ (“nyoo”)
- American new = /nuː/ (“noo”)

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://dictionary.cambridge.org\&sz=32)

Pronunciation in English

That gives us a natural first transition. Later, we move from noo into noh, then Australian naur, then now, and finally meow.

