## 3. The final phonetic mutation

This is the difficult and interesting part.

PROPOSED SOUND GRADIENT

The critical pivot is nootropic. Its standard pronunciation begins with noh, not noo, so it actually belongs close to the end rather than with the nuclear/noodle group.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://dictionary.cambridge.org\&sz=32)

Cambridge Dictionary



Then introduce words beginning with the ordinary English no sound:

Noah · Nobel · Noble · Nova · Nolan · No · Know · Known · Knows · Nope · Nose · Note · Notice · Notorious · Nootropic · No-one · Nobody · No way

These can gradually shift from /noʊ/ toward a more Australian-sounding vowel.

The Australian naur meme is especially useful because the pronunciation becomes the entire joke: H2O: Just Add Water was a prominent early source of the meme.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://knowyourmeme.com\&sz=32)

Know Your Meme



After that, two possible endings:

Ending A — Gradual absurdity

No → Naur → Now → Mao → Maui → Meowth → Meow

Vowels open up, then the initial N changes into an M.

Ending B — Perfect linguistic trick

New → Mew → Meow

“New” and “Mew” differ by just one initial consonant in British pronunciation.

I prefer Ending A for the montage. Ending B is the cleaner linguistic transformation, but A has much greater comic escalation.

## 4. How I'd edit the complete sequence

Start almost ridiculously normal: an old television commercial shouting “NEW!”, immediately followed by a serious actor exclaiming “I KNEW IT!”, then a Fox News anchor, then Monty Python's newt.

Every cut changes context completely, but the opening sound remains.

Keep a fairly regular pulse, perhaps 0.3–0.7 seconds per clip, with occasional longer clips for recognisable comedic lines. Move from advertisements and sitcoms into science documentaries, news, politicians, gaming and children's programmes.

As you approach the no section, introduce competing pronunciations. One voice says “noob,” another “nootropic,” then a succession of people saying “no.” Darth Vader gets a disproportionately dramatic moment.

Then an Australian girl says “naur.”

One more person says “now.”

Moana says “Maui.”

Meowth speaks.

And finally, an actual cat meows.

Cut to silence.

The last three seconds should feel completely inevitable despite having no logical connection to the opening commercial.

One additional word worth using is gnu: the animal's name is pronounced exactly like American new. That gives you the opportunity for a sudden wildlife-documentary cut long before the final cat.&#x20;

